              "section": "Who NAD+ Is For and Who Should Avoid It",
              "subsection": "",
              "excerpt": "Active malignancy — NAD+ supports cellular proliferation, including potentially in cancer cells; oncologist consultation is essential.",
              "truncated": false
            },
            {
              "section": "Who NAD+ Is For and Who Should Avoid It",
              "subsection": "",
              "excerpt": "People on glucose-lowering medications — NAD+ may enhance insulin sensitivity over time; closer glucose monitoring is prudent.",
              "truncated": false
            },
            {
              "section": "NAD+ Side Effects & Safety",
              "subsection": "Commonly reported with IV",
              "excerpt": "Flushing and warmth, especially with faster infusion rates.",
              "truncated": false
            },
            {
              "section": "NAD+ Side Effects & Safety",
              "subsection": "Commonly reported with IV",
              "excerpt": "Chest tightness or shortness of breath at faster infusion rates.",
              "truncated": false
            },
            {
              "section": "NAD+ Side Effects & Safety",
              "subsection": "Theoretical or longer-horizon concerns",
              "excerpt": "Active malignancy is a contraindication because NAD+ supports cellular proliferation.",
              "truncated": false
            },
            {
              "section": "NAD+ Side Effects & Safety",
              "subsection": "Theoretical or longer-horizon concerns",
              "excerpt": "Most acute side effects are dose-dependent and rate-dependent. Slower injection, lower starting doses, and morning timing all reduce reported tolerability issues.",
              "truncated": false
            }
